          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 770T: figures and conflicts</h3>
          <p>
            The 770T owner's manual lists 0.5 to 12.4 mph, 0 to 15% incline, a 400 lb maximum user, an
            assembled length of 84 inches, a width of 35.6 inches, a height of 62.5 inches and a product weight
            of 410 lb. Retailer listings agree on the speed and user weight, add a 5 HP peak AC drive motor and
            a 1,000 lb-thrust incline motor, and give 82.5 by 36 by 59 inches and 500 lb. The difference may
            reflect the console fitted or a shipping weight, but we cannot tell, so neither figure is on the
            card. We found no reliable running-surface figure; measure the belt on any unit you view.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The R Series: figures and conflicts</h3>
          <p>
            Life Fitness's catalogue page for the R Series lists a 4.0 HP continuous, 8.0 HP peak AC motor,
            400 lb, an 8.5-inch step-up and a dedicated 20-amp circuit at 120 V. It also gives two different
            surface sizes: a 22 by 60-inch belt and a 21 by 60-inch running area. That is not necessarily a
            mistake — the belt is wider than the usable surface between the side rails — and Cybex's sell sheet
            and an independent review both give 21 by 60 inches. Speed (14 mph) and incline (0 to 15%) come
            from the sell sheet and that review; Life Fitness's page did not list them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

